\details{
\strong{One fit, one interval construction}. \code{"wald"} is the
default for every backend because otherwise one object could carry several
constructions: an \code{lme4} fit's printed table would carry profile-likelihood
intervals while the effect-size layer reported Wald ones for the same
coefficient. The critical value is the same one the effects layer uses, so
the two agree by construction rather than by coincidence.

The reference distribution is the one the backend's own inference
supports. For a \code{stats}-backed fit it is the exact t distribution, and
the result is identical to \code{stats::confint.lm()}. \code{lme4} reports no
denominator degrees of freedom for a fixed effect, and its own Wald
interval (\code{confint(fit, method = "Wald")}) uses the normal quantile, so
the default interval on an \code{lme4} fit is identical to that one.
\code{sna::lnam} estimates by maximum likelihood with asymptotically normal
inference (its own \code{summary()} reports z values), so it gets the normal
quantile too. \code{sna} has no \code{confint()} method to delegate to.

On an \code{lme4} fit \code{method = "profile"} gives the profile interval, a better
interval where it can be computed. It is not the default because it would be
recomputed on every \code{print()}, \code{summary()} and \code{tidy()} of the same object.

\code{AIC()}/\code{BIC()} are computed from the fit's own log-likelihood, with its
\code{df} and \code{nobs} attributes, on every backend. \code{sna::lnam} has no \code{logLik()}
method, so that value is assembled from the model log-likelihood and degrees of
freedom it returns. \code{NA} if the backend could not produce a valid log-likelihood.
